window.AGRIBASE_CONTENT.articles.push(
{slug:'water-tomato-plants',title:'How to Water Tomato Plants',category:'Irrigation',description:'Water tomato roots consistently while reducing runoff, leaf wetness and waste.',body:'<p>Tomatoes need a consistent root-zone supply, but the correct amount depends on soil, weather, plant size and irrigation method. Check moisture below the surface before watering again.</p><h2>Practical routine</h2><ul><li>Water the soil rather than the leaves.</li><li>Inspect the root zone and adjust for heat, wind and rainfall.</li><li>Use mulch where suitable to reduce evaporation.</li><li>Keep records of irrigation and plant response.</li></ul><p>Flowering and fruit set are sensitive stages, but avoid treating one schedule as universal.</p>'},
{slug:'tomato-pests-diseases',title:'Common Tomato Pests and Diseases',category:'Pest & Disease Management',description:'Scout tomato plants systematically and identify the problem before choosing a response.',body:'<p>Look at new growth, leaf undersides, stems, roots and fruit. Record the pattern, affected area, weather and recent inputs.</p><h2>Good first steps</h2><ol><li>Confirm whether the cause is an insect, disease, nutrient issue, water stress or physical damage.</li><li>Remove badly affected material where appropriate and improve airflow.</li><li>Use resistant varieties and sanitation as part of prevention.</li><li>Follow local product labels and pre-harvest intervals if treatment is needed.</li></ol>'},
{slug:'plan-fertilizer-application',title:'How to Plan Fertilizer Application',category:'Soil & Fertility',description:'Build a fertilizer plan from soil information, crop stage, product analysis and records.',body:'<p>A responsible plan starts with a soil test and a realistic yield target. Split applications where crop demand and the irrigation system make that practical.</p><h2>Check before applying</h2><ul><li>Crop and growth stage</li><li>Soil pH and nutrient results</li><li>Product analysis and application area</li><li>Rain forecast, placement and safety requirements</li></ul><p>Do not infer a local rate from a generic example. Keep the label and farm record with the application.</p>'},
{slug:'reduce-water-waste',title:'How to Reduce Water Waste on the Farm',category:'Irrigation',description:'Reduce losses by measuring delivery, improving soil cover and managing timing.',body:'<p>Start with a simple water audit: source volume, field delivery, leaks, runoff and crop response. Fix measurable losses before expanding irrigation.</p><h2>Low-cost actions</h2><ul><li>Repair leaks and blocked emitters.</li><li>Use soil cover and maintain drainage without allowing runoff.</li><li>Group crops by water need where possible.</li><li>Measure irrigation output and compare it with weather and soil observations.</li></ul>'},
{slug:'simple-farm-budget',title:'How to Create a Simple Farm Budget',category:'Farm Business',description:'List planned costs and income before planting, then compare the plan with actual records.',body:'<p>A budget is a planning document, not a promise. List quantities, unit prices and timing for seed, fertilizer, labour, water, transport, packaging and other costs.</p><h2>Use three scenarios</h2><p>Test lower yield, expected yield and a weaker selling price. Add a contingency for costs you can reasonably anticipate. Review the budget weekly during the season.</p>'},
{slug:'livestock-feed-planning',title:'Livestock Feed Planning Basics',category:'Livestock',description:'Plan feed around animal type, production stage, forage availability, water and safe storage.',body:'<p>Feed requirements vary with species, age, body size, pregnancy, growth and production. Start by identifying the animals and recording available forage and purchased feed.</p><h2>Practical checks</h2><ul><li>Provide clean water and safe, dry storage.</li><li>Change rations gradually and watch intake and body condition.</li><li>Use a qualified nutritionist or extension officer for formulated rations.</li><li>Record feed quantities, cost and animal response.</li></ul>'},
{slug:'farming-dry-conditions',title:'Farming During Dry Conditions',category:'Farm Planning',description:'Prioritize water, soil cover, crop choice and records when rainfall is limited.',body:'<p>Dry conditions require a plan based on available water and likely weather, not optimism alone. Protect priority fields and livestock first.</p><h2>Plan carefully</h2><ul><li>Prioritize crops and growth stages that are most sensitive to stress.</li><li>Reduce evaporation with soil cover and timely weed control.</li><li>Review planting windows and varieties with local advice.</li><li>Keep livestock water and feed contingency records.</li></ul>'},
{slug:'beginner-farming-mistakes',title:'Common Mistakes Beginner Farmers Make',category:'Beginner Farming',description:'Avoid preventable losses through better planning, records, scouting and market checks.',body:'<p>Common problems include planting without a water plan, buying inputs without a budget, ignoring soil constraints, crowding crops and failing to record costs.</p><h2>A better sequence</h2><p>Start with the market and site, test or observe the soil, plan water, choose a suitable crop and record each activity. Review results honestly after harvest.</p>'},
{slug:'plan-vegetable-garden',title:'How to Plan a Vegetable Garden',category:'Farm Planning',description:'Organize beds, crop choice, succession, water, paths and harvest timing for a productive garden.',body:'<p>Map the available space, sun, slope, water point and paths. Group crops by water need and plan enough access for weeding and harvest.</p><h2>Make the plan useful</h2><p>Choose crops your household or market will use, stagger plantings where the climate allows and rotate related crop families when practical. Leave space for composting and safe storage.</p>'},
{slug:'understanding-crop-spacing',title:'Understanding Crop Spacing',category:'Crop Production',description:'Choose spacing with variety, equipment, airflow, soil fertility and market size in mind.',body:'<p>Closer spacing can change competition, airflow, disease risk and harvest size. Wider spacing can improve access but may reduce plants per area.</p><h2>Use evidence</h2><p>Start with local variety guidance, then compare a small trial. Measure plant population, harvestable yield and quality rather than judging spacing by appearance alone.</p>'},
{slug:'farm-planning-basics',title:'Farm Planning Basics',category:'Farm Planning',description:'Turn a farm idea into a sequence of site, crop, water, labour, market and record decisions.',body:'<p>Good planning connects the field to the buyer. Write down the crop, area, timing, inputs, labour, water source, expected yield, likely price and major risks.</p><h2>Review points</h2><p>Review before planting, after establishment, at first harvest and after the season. Use the result to improve the next plan rather than hiding an unprofitable assumption.</p>'},
{slug:'crop-rotation-guide',title:'Crop Rotation: Why It Matters and How to Plan It',category:'Soil & Fertility',description:'Learn how to rotate crop families, break pest and disease cycles, protect soil and build practical 3- and 4-year sequences.',body:'<p>Crop rotation means growing different crop families in a planned order on the same piece of land over successive seasons. It is one of the simplest ways to make a field more resilient. A good rotation reduces the chance that the same pests, diseases and nutrient problems meet the same host crop year after year.</p><div class="notice"><strong>Core rule:</strong> rotate the crop family, not just the crop name. Tomatoes, peppers, potatoes and eggplant are all in the nightshade family, so planting one after another is not a real break for many shared pests and diseases.</div><h2>Why crop rotation is important</h2><ul><li><strong>Breaks pest cycles:</strong> many insects and soil pests are most successful when their preferred host returns to the same field repeatedly.</li><li><strong>Reduces disease carryover:</strong> crop residues and volunteer plants can carry pathogens. Moving to a non-host crop gives some diseases less opportunity to multiply.</li><li><strong>Balances nutrient demand:</strong> heavy-feeding crops can be followed by crops with different nutrient needs. Legumes can add biologically fixed nitrogen when they grow well and are managed correctly.</li><li><strong>Improves soil structure:</strong> cereals, grasses, deep-rooted crops, fibrous roots and cover crops explore the soil differently and can improve aggregation and infiltration.</li><li><strong>Suppresses weeds:</strong> changing planting dates, row spacing, canopy shape and cultivation timing makes it harder for one weed community to dominate.</li><li><strong>Spreads financial risk:</strong> different crops can spread harvest dates, labour demand, market exposure and weather risk.</li><li><strong>Improves decision-making:</strong> rotation records show which crops, residues and field conditions came before the current crop.</li></ul><h2>Think in crop families</h2><p>Make a simple family map before planning. Common groups include nightshades such as tomato, pepper, potato and eggplant; brassicas such as cabbage, broccoli, cauliflower and kale; legumes such as beans, peas, cowpeas and groundnuts; cucurbits such as pumpkin, squash, cucumber and melon; cereals and grasses such as maize, sorghum, millet, wheat and oats; and alliums such as onion, garlic and leek. Carrots, beetroot and other roots are useful separate groups in many plans, but check local disease risks.</p><p>Rotation length depends on the crop, disease, pest, climate, soil and farm system. A short rotation is better than no plan, but a two-year break may not be enough for long-lived soil pests or diseases. Aim for three or four years between related crops where land allows, and follow local extension advice when a serious disease or nematode problem is present.</p><h2>How to build a rotation</h2><ol><li><strong>Draw the field:</strong> divide the farm into manageable blocks and label drainage, slope, soil type, irrigation and previous problems.</li><li><strong>List the crop families:</strong> group planned crops by family rather than by market name.</li><li><strong>Record the previous two to four seasons:</strong> include crop, variety, planting date, harvest date, fertilizer, manure, herbicides, disease, pests and yield.</li><li><strong>Place the highest-risk crops carefully:</strong> do not follow a crop with a close relative just because the field is convenient.</li><li><strong>Add a soil-building phase:</strong> use a legume, grass, mixed cover crop, pasture or fallow period where practical.</li><li><strong>Check water and labour:</strong> a rotation must fit available irrigation, equipment, labour peaks and market timing.</li><li><strong>Review before planting:</strong> compare the plan with soil test results, seed availability, weather outlook and current pest pressure.</li></ol><h2>Useful 3-year examples</h2><p><strong>Example A - vegetable field:</strong> Year 1 maize or another cereal, Year 2 beans or cowpeas, Year 3 cabbage or another brassica. Put tomatoes, potatoes and peppers in a different block or after a longer break from nightshades.</p><p><strong>Example B - irrigated market garden:</strong> Year 1 tomato, Year 2 beans, Year 3 cabbage, Year 4 onion or carrot. This separates nightshades from brassicas and uses a legume between demanding vegetable crops.</p><p><strong>Example C - dryland field:</strong> Year 1 sorghum or millet, Year 2 cowpea, Year 3 sunflower or a locally suitable broadleaf crop. Choose crops that match rainfall and keep soil covered after harvest.</p><h2>Useful 4-year example</h2><p>A balanced sequence can be: <strong>1. cereal or grass, 2. legume, 3. brassica or leafy vegetable, 4. root crop or cucurbit.</strong> A farmer may then return to the cereal, provided the field is checked for weeds, pests, disease and fertility changes. The exact order can change: avoid placing a crop after a family that shares its main disease, and place the crop with the greatest market or disease risk where the field conditions are best.</p><h2>Small garden rotation</h2><p>In a small garden, divide the space into three or four beds and move families one bed each season. For example, Bed 1 can hold tomatoes and peppers, Bed 2 cabbage and kale, Bed 3 beans and peas, and Bed 4 carrots, onions and beetroot. Next season, move each group to the next bed. Keep a written map because memory becomes unreliable after several seasons.</p><h2>Cover crops, residues and manure</h2><ul><li>Choose cover crops for the purpose: legumes for nitrogen contribution, grasses for biomass and soil cover, and mixtures for several goals.</li><li>Do not assume every legume fixes the same amount of nitrogen. Inoculation, soil pH, moisture, variety and nodulation affect performance.</li><li>Manage cover crops before they compete with the next crop or set seed. Leave enough time for residues to break down where they could delay planting or tie up nitrogen.</li><li>Remove or destroy diseased residues according to local advice. Compost only material that will be safely composted; do not spread infected residue around the farm.</li><li>Use well-managed manure and compost. Fresh manure can burn plants, add weed seeds or create food-safety risks, so follow local rules and appropriate waiting periods.</li></ul><h2>When rotation is not enough</h2><p>Rotation cannot correct every problem. It will not replace clean seed, resistant varieties, sanitation, drainage, scouting, soil testing, safe irrigation or correct pesticide use. Some pests have wide host ranges, some weeds reproduce from persistent seed, and wind or irrigation water can move disease between fields. For severe nematodes, wilt, clubroot or other persistent problems, get a local diagnosis and plan the break period with an agronomist or extension officer.</p><h2>Common rotation mistakes</h2><ul><li>Rotating tomato to potato and calling it a break.</li><li>Ignoring volunteer plants and weeds that keep a pest or disease host alive.</li><li>Planting the same family in nearby beds where roots, tools, water or residues can move the problem.</li><li>Using a legume without checking whether it shares an important disease with the next crop.</li><li>Choosing a sequence that looks good on paper but creates an impossible labour, water or harvest workload.</li><li>Changing crops without recording what was applied to the field, especially herbicides with carryover restrictions.</li></ul><h2>Keep a rotation record</h2><p>For every field or bed, record the season, crop family, variety, planting and harvest dates, fertilizer and manure, irrigation, pest and disease observations, weed problems, yield and next action. Review the record after harvest. A rotation is a working plan that improves with evidence, not a fixed rule that ignores local conditions.</p><div class="notice"><strong>Practical takeaway:</strong> start with the field history, separate related crop families, include a soil-building phase, protect the break with weed and residue management, and adapt the sequence to your soil, climate, water, market and local advice.</div>'}
,{slug:'understanding-your-soil',title:'Understanding Your Soil: A Practical Guide for Better Farming',category:'Soil & Fertility',description:'A complete field guide to soil texture, structure, fertility, pH, water, slope, erosion and practical soil conservation.',body:'<p>Use this complete practical guide to investigate soil and connect observations and soil tests to crop, fertilizer, irrigation and conservation decisions.</p><p><a href="/guides/understanding-your-soil.html">Open the full soil guide.</a></p>'}
);

const fertilizerHandbook = window.AGRIBASE_CONTENT.articles.find(article => article.slug === 'plan-fertilizer-application');
if (fertilizerHandbook) {
	fertilizerHandbook.title = 'Fertilizer and Nutrient Management: A Practical Farmer Handbook';
	fertilizerHandbook.category = 'Soil & Fertility';
	fertilizerHandbook.description = 'Understand plant nutrients, soil pH, fertilizer products, the 4Rs, nutrient losses and low-cost field decisions.';
	fertilizerHandbook.body = `
<div class="notice"><strong>How to use this handbook:</strong> Read it from the beginning to build the ideas, or jump to a nutrient, soil condition, fertilizer product, troubleshooting scenario or checklist. The examples explain the method; they are not universal rates. Soil tests, crop stage, local recommendations, product labels, water quality and field history must guide the final decision.</div>
<h2>Contents</h2>
<ol><li><a href="#introduction">Introduction and the farmer's question</a></li><li><a href="#nutrition">What plant nutrition means</a></li><li><a href="#nutrients">Essential nutrients</a></li><li><a href="#movement">How nutrients move in soil and reach roots</a></li><li><a href="#availability">Nutrient availability and interactions</a></li><li><a href="#products">Fertilizer sources and label calculations</a></li><li><a href="#four-rs">The 4Rs: source, rate, time and place</a></li><li><a href="#losses">Fertilizer losses</a></li><li><a href="#diagnosis">Deficiency diagnosis and troubleshooting</a></li><li><a href="#ph">Soil pH, acidity and lime</a></li><li><a href="#alkaline">Alkaline soil</a></li><li><a href="#saline">Saline soil</a></li><li><a href="#sodic">Sodic soil</a></li><li><a href="#crusting">Soil crusting or soil cap</a></li><li><a href="#hardpan">Soil pan or hardpan</a></li><li><a href="#organic">Organic matter</a></li><li><a href="#low-cost">Low-cost fertilizer management</a></li><li><a href="#locations">Location-aware examples and unit conversion</a></li><li><a href="#scenarios">Farmer scenarios</a></li><li><a href="#checklist">Final farmer checklist</a></li></ol>

<h2 id="introduction">1. Introduction: fertilizer is a tool, not a guarantee</h2>
<p>A crop needs light, water, air, a healthy root system, suitable temperature and a supply of essential nutrients. Fertilizer supplies one or more nutrients, but it cannot repair every problem in a field. A plant may remain yellow after fertilizer because the wrong nutrient was applied, the roots are damaged, the soil is too dry or wet, the pH is restricting uptake, or the fertilizer was lost. The first economical question is therefore not "Which product should I buy?" It is "What is limiting this crop, and what evidence will help me choose an action?"</p>
<p>Good nutrient management connects the soil, fertilizer, water, roots, plant leaves, weather and farm records. It aims for profitable yield and healthy soil, not the largest possible fertilizer bill.</p>

<h2 id="nutrition">2. What plant nutrition means</h2>
<p><strong>Plant nutrition</strong> is the supply, movement, uptake and use of chemical elements that a plant needs to grow, reproduce and complete its life cycle. Plants obtain carbon, hydrogen and oxygen mainly from air and water. They obtain mineral nutrients from the soil solution, from exchange sites on soil particles and, in some systems, from foliar sprays or fertigation. Roots do not take up a dry fertilizer granule as a granule. They take up ions dissolved in water, or small molecules in solution, through living root surfaces.</p>
<p>Common examples are nitrate (NO3-) and ammonium (NH4+) for nitrogen, phosphate ions for phosphorus, potassium ions (K+), calcium ions (Ca2+), magnesium ions (Mg2+) and sulfate (SO4 2-) for sulfur. The exact form available changes with pH, oxygen, moisture, microbes and the soil minerals.</p>
<h3>Soil nutrient versus available nutrient</h3>
<p><strong>Total nutrient quantity</strong> is everything present in the soil, including nutrients locked inside minerals, organic matter or strongly held compounds. <strong>Available nutrient</strong> is the portion that can reach roots and be taken up at the rate the crop needs under the current conditions. A soil can contain a large total amount of iron but still show iron deficiency if high pH changes it into a form roots cannot access. A soil can contain phosphorus but fix it onto iron, aluminium or calcium compounds. A sandy soil can have nitrogen available today and lose it below the root zone after heavy rain tomorrow.</p>
<p>Water dissolves nutrients and carries some of them toward roots. Soil particles and organic matter hold other nutrients and release them gradually. Microorganisms transform organic forms into mineral forms and change nitrogen, sulfur and phosphorus cycling. Roots release compounds, take up ions and alter pH and oxygen around their surfaces. This is why "I applied fertilizer, so why are my plants still deficient?" has several possible answers:</p>
<ul><li>The test or visual diagnosis was wrong, so the product supplied a nutrient the plant did not need.</li><li>The soil pH, salinity, sodicity or temperature made the nutrient unavailable.</li><li>The soil was too dry for ions to move, or too wet for roots to receive oxygen.</li><li>Roots were pruned by compaction, insects, disease, waterlogging or fertilizer burn.</li><li>The nutrient moved below the root zone, ran off, volatilized, became fixed or was tied up temporarily by microbes.</li><li>The rate, timing or placement did not match crop demand, or the product was poor quality or incorrectly measured.</li><li>The symptom is actually drought, herbicide injury, pest damage or disease.</li></ul>
<p>Investigate before applying more. More fertilizer can increase salt stress, nutrient imbalance, cost and pollution without correcting the limiting factor.</p>

<h2 id="nutrients">3. Essential nutrients: what each one does and how to manage it</h2>
<p>Plants need 17 elements for normal growth: carbon, hydrogen and oxygen, plus nitrogen, phosphorus, potassium, sulfur, calcium, magnesium, iron, zinc, manganese, copper, boron, molybdenum, chlorine and nickel. The word <strong>essential</strong> means that without the element the plant cannot complete its life cycle and another element cannot fully replace its function. The amount required does not determine its importance. A micronutrient is needed in a smaller quantity, but too little or too much can still reduce yield.</p>

<h2>Macronutrients</h2>
<h3>Nitrogen (N)</h3>
<p>Nitrogen is part of amino acids, proteins, chlorophyll, enzymes and genetic material. It supports leaf area, photosynthesis, canopy growth, tillering and grain or fruit production. Plants mainly absorb nitrate (NO3-) and ammonium (NH4+), with small amounts of organic nitrogen in special conditions. Nitrate moves readily with soil water by mass flow and leaching; ammonium is held more strongly on cation exchange sites but can later convert to nitrate.</p>
<p>Nitrogen is mobile inside the plant, so shortage usually appears first on older lower leaves: uniform pale green to yellow colour, slow growth, thin stems and early leaf ageing. Yield falls through fewer leaves, flowers, grains or fruit, but excess N can delay maturity, make soft lush growth, increase lodging and pest or disease risk, and reduce fruit quality. Causes include low organic matter, leaching in sandy soil, erosion, inadequate mineralization, poor roots, waterlogging and competition from microbes decomposing high-carbon residue.</p>
<p>High pH and warm moist soil can increase ammonia loss from surface urea. Waterlogging can cause denitrification, in which microbes convert nitrate to gases that leave the soil. Dry soil limits movement and root uptake even when the test shows nitrogen. Split applications, placement near active roots, incorporation or timely rainfall/irrigation, and matching application to crop demand improve efficiency. Urea, ammonium nitrate, CAN, ammonium sulfate, manures, compost and NPK products supply N, but each also brings different nutrients, acidity effects, salt risk, cost and handling requirements. Do not apply N to dry bare soil and assume heat alone always causes loss: the risk depends on surface pH, residue, wind, temperature, urease activity and whether rain or irrigation moves urea into soil. Avoid direct contact with seed and use the product label.</p>

<h3>Phosphorus (P)</h3>
<p>Phosphorus is involved in energy transfer, ATP, genetic material, membranes, root exploration, early establishment, flowering and seed formation. Roots absorb phosphate ions, mainly H2PO4- and HPO4 2-, depending on pH. Phosphorus is relatively immobile in soil. It moves mostly a short distance by diffusion through water films and by root interception, so a root must grow near the available phosphate. Clay, iron and aluminium minerals can fix phosphate in acid soils; calcium compounds can reduce availability in calcareous or high-pH soils.</p>
<p>Deficiency often appears first in older leaves because P can move within the plant. Plants may be stunted, slow to mature, dark green or purplish in some crops, with weak roots, delayed flowering and poor seed or grain filling. Cold soil, compaction, dry soil, waterlogging, extreme pH, low organic matter and low root activity can cause symptoms even after fertilizer was applied. Banding or placing a modest amount beside and below the seed or developing root zone can be more efficient than broadcasting on a soil that fixes P strongly. Keep concentrated fertilizer away from seed because salts or free ammonia from some products can injure germinating roots.</p>
<p>DAP (18-46-0), MAP (11-52-0), TSP, SSP, NPK products, manure and compost can supply P. DAP also supplies N and creates a temporarily alkaline zone; MAP supplies less N and creates a more acidic zone. Organic P becomes available through decomposition, but slowly and variably. Excess P wastes money, can induce zinc or iron deficiency and can move with eroded soil into water, causing algal growth. Use soil tests, crop removal, pH management and placement rather than treating every poor root system as a request for more P.</p>

<h3>Potassium (K)</h3>
<p>Potassium regulates water balance, stomatal opening, enzyme activation, sugar movement, stem strength, disease and drought response, and grain or fruit filling. Plants absorb K+ from soil solution. It moves to roots mainly by diffusion and mass flow, and roots intercept some. Clay minerals and organic matter hold exchangeable K; sandy soils often hold less and can leach it. Some clay fixes K between layers, making total K much larger than available K.</p>
<p>K is mobile in the plant, so deficiency commonly starts on older leaves as yellowing or scorching along edges and tips, followed by weak stems, lodging, poor water regulation and uneven filling. Causes include low exchange capacity, heavy crop removal, leaching, drought-restricted roots, high calcium or magnesium competition in some situations, and heavy applications of other cations. MOP or potassium chloride (0-0-60) is concentrated and affordable but adds chloride and salt. SOP or potassium sulfate (0-0-50 + sulfur) supplies K and sulfur with less chloride but usually costs more. Manure, compost and NPK products contribute variable K.</p>
<p>Place K according to the crop, soil test, salt sensitivity and product. Avoid a concentrated band against seed or roots. Split large rates on leachable soils where practical. Excess K can reduce magnesium or calcium uptake, raise salinity and reduce profit; it does not automatically improve drought tolerance if roots cannot access water.</p>

<h3>Calcium (Ca)</h3>
<p>Calcium strengthens cell walls and membranes, supports root and shoot growing points, pollen development and signalling. Roots absorb Ca2+ from soil water, mainly by mass flow and root interception because Ca moves with transpiration. It is relatively immobile in the plant, so deficiency appears first in young leaves, growing points, roots or fruit tissues: distorted new growth, necrotic tips, poor roots, blossom-end rot in tomato and pepper, or bitter pit in apple. Blossom-end rot is not always simply low soil calcium; uneven water supply can stop calcium delivery to fruit.</p>
<p>Acid soils may have low exchangeable Ca and aluminium toxicity. Excess potassium, magnesium or ammonium can compete with Ca uptake. Dry soil, damaged roots and low transpiration reduce delivery. Lime supplies calcium carbonate and raises pH; dolomitic lime supplies calcium and magnesium and suits a confirmed magnesium need. Gypsum supplies calcium and sulfur without raising pH much and can help certain sodic soils, but it is not a universal cure for low pH or every calcium symptom. Calcium nitrate supplies soluble Ca and N but is usually a targeted input, not a reason to ignore irrigation and root health.</p>

<h3>Magnesium (Mg)</h3>
<p>Magnesium is the central atom in chlorophyll and activates enzymes involved in photosynthesis, energy use and carbohydrate movement. Plants absorb Mg2+, which moves toward roots in soil water and is held on exchange sites. It is mobile inside the plant, so deficiency often begins on older leaves as interveinal yellowing: the veins remain greener while tissue between them fades, then leaves may redden or drop.</p>
<p>Acid, sandy or heavily leached soils may be low in Mg. Excess potassium, calcium or ammonium can compete with Mg. Dryness and poor roots reduce uptake. Dolomitic lime corrects acidity while supplying Mg where a test supports it; kieserite or magnesium sulfate supplies Mg and sulfur; soluble foliar products can give temporary help but do not repair a large soil shortage. Excess Mg can interfere with Ca and K balance. Prevent it with soil testing, balanced liming, organic matter and avoiding repeated one-sided K applications.</p>

<h3>Sulfur (S)</h3>
<p>Sulfur is part of sulfur-containing amino acids, proteins, enzymes, oils and plant defence compounds. Roots mainly absorb sulfate (SO4 2-), which moves with water by mass flow and can leach, especially in sandy soil. Some organic sulfur must be mineralized by microbes before plants can use it. Sulfur is relatively immobile in the plant, so young leaves may become pale green to yellow while older leaves remain greener. Growth, protein formation, nodulation and oilseed quality can suffer.</p>
<p>Losses increase where atmospheric sulfur deposition is low, high-analysis fertilizers contain no S, residues are removed, soil organic matter is low or heavy rain leaches sulfate. Acid sulfate soils are a separate hazard and need specialist interpretation. Ammonium sulfate, gypsum, SOP, elemental sulfur, manure, compost and sulfur-containing NPK products supply S. Elemental sulfur must be oxidized by microbes and works slowly; sulfate is immediately available but can leach. Do not use sulfur simply to force a pH change without a test. Nitrogen and sulfur must be balanced because a crop cannot use high N efficiently to make protein when S is limiting.</p>

<h2>Micronutrients</h2>
<h3>Iron (Fe)</h3>
<p>Iron supports chlorophyll formation, electron transfer, respiration and enzyme activity. Plants absorb Fe2+, Fe3+ chelates and other soluble or chelated forms. Fe is relatively immobile in the plant, so deficiency appears on young leaves as interveinal chlorosis with green veins, often in high-pH, calcareous, waterlogged, cold or compacted soil. Roots acidify their surroundings and release compounds that help acquire Fe, but this may not overcome severe conditions.</p>
<p>High pH, excess phosphorus, poor aeration, bicarbonate-rich irrigation water and damaged roots reduce Fe availability. Organic matter can form useful complexes, but organic inputs are not guaranteed to supply enough Fe. Correct the underlying pH, drainage or root problem first. Soil-applied chelated Fe, iron sulfate, foliar Fe or acidifying management may help depending on the crop and diagnosis. Unnecessary iron can waste money and stain or burn leaves; never confuse Fe chlorosis with N deficiency, which is usually more uniform and begins on older leaves.</p>

<h3>Zinc (Zn)</h3>
<p>Zinc activates enzymes, supports growth hormones, internode development, pollen and protein formation. Roots take up Zn2+ and soluble complexes, mainly by diffusion and root contact. Zn is relatively immobile in plants, so young leaves may be small, distorted or chlorotic, with short internodes and striping; maize may show pale bands. High pH, high phosphorus, cold wet soil, erosion of topsoil, low organic matter and sandy soil can reduce Zn. Excess P can induce Zn deficiency even when soil Zn is not very low.</p>
<p>Use a soil test before applying zinc sulfate, zinc oxide, chelated Zn, fortified NPK, manure or a foliar product. Soil placement near roots can be effective; foliar sprays are small corrective treatments and must follow crop label concentration. Zinc toxicity can damage roots and leaves and interfere with iron, so more is not safer.</p>

<h3>Manganese (Mn)</h3>
<p>Manganese activates enzymes and supports photosynthesis, nitrogen metabolism and oxygen release in photosystem II. Plants absorb Mn2+ by diffusion and mass flow. Mn becomes less soluble in well-aerated high-pH soils and more soluble in acid, waterlogged or reducing soil. It is relatively immobile in the plant, so young leaves show interveinal chlorosis, grey speckling or small necrotic spots, though patterns vary by crop.</p>
<p>Liming an already high-pH soil, cool wet conditions, high organic matter binding and sandy leaching can reduce Mn uptake. Confirm with soil or tissue testing because iron and magnesium symptoms overlap. Manganese sulfate, chelates or foliar products may correct a confirmed shortage. Excess Mn is toxic in strongly acid soil; pH and drainage management are safer long-term prevention than repeated sprays.</p>

<h3>Copper (Cu)</h3>
<p>Copper supports enzymes, lignin formation, photosynthesis, pollen fertility and disease defence. Roots absorb Cu2+ and soluble complexes, but much Cu is strongly held by organic matter and clay. It is relatively immobile in the plant, so new leaves may be pale, twisted or wilted, stems weak and flowering or grain set poor. High organic matter, high pH, sandy leaching and strong fixation can lower availability; low pH can increase toxicity.</p>
<p>Use copper sulfate, chelated Cu or fortified fertilizer only after diagnosis. Soil application is longer lasting; foliar correction is small and must avoid leaf burn. Copper accumulates because it is not readily lost, so excessive applications can harm roots and soil organisms and reduce iron or zinc uptake. Test manure, compost or repeated copper spray history where relevant.</p>

<h3>Boron (B)</h3>
<p>Boron supports cell wall formation, growing points, pollen germination, flowering, seed set and sugar movement. Plants absorb boric acid and borate forms, mostly moving with water by mass flow. Boron is relatively immobile in many crops, so young growing points, flowers, pods, roots or fruits are affected first: distorted tips, hollow stems or roots, poor pollination and cracked or misshapen produce. Symptoms vary greatly by crop.</p>
<p>Boron deficiency is more likely in sandy, low-organic-matter, dry or strongly alkaline soils. Availability can increase in acid soils, but too much B is toxic and the safe range between shortage and toxicity is narrow. Use a soil or tissue test and a carefully measured boron source such as borax, boric acid or fortified fertilizer. Place uniformly and never guess a handful rate. Irrigation and organic matter help maintain a steady supply; heavy rainfall can leach B.</p>

<h3>Molybdenum (Mo)</h3>
<p>Molybdenum is needed for nitrate reductase and nitrogen fixation, so it is important for nitrate use and legumes. Plants absorb molybdate ions. Mo is relatively mobile in the plant and often becomes more available as pH rises, while acid soils can restrict it. Deficiency can resemble nitrogen shortage: pale leaves, weak growth, poor nodulation or whiptail in cauliflower. Lime may correct acid-soil Mo shortage indirectly, and tiny amounts of molybdate seed treatment, soil or foliar product may help where tests and local advice support it.</p>
<p>Do not confuse Mo with a request for general N. Excess Mo can create molybdenum toxicity in plants and copper imbalance in grazing livestock. Use measured products and keep records.</p>

<h3>Chlorine (Cl)</h3>
<p>Chlorine is involved in charge balance, water movement, photosynthesis and stomatal function. Plants absorb chloride (Cl-) from soil water. It moves easily with water and is mobile in the plant. Most soils receive enough from rain, irrigation, manure or potassium chloride. Deficiency is uncommon but may cause wilting, pale leaves and poor roots; excess chloride causes leaf burn, salt stress and quality problems in chloride-sensitive crops such as some tobacco, potato or berry systems.</p>
<p>MOP supplies K and chloride, while SOP supplies K and sulfur with little chloride. Choose based on crop sensitivity, soil EC, irrigation water and price. Leaching requires good drainage and must not move salts into groundwater.</p>

<h3>Nickel (Ni)</h3>
<p>Nickel is needed in tiny amounts for urease, the enzyme that lets plants use urea-derived nitrogen, and for seed viability and nitrogen metabolism. Plants absorb Ni2+ and soluble complexes. It can move in soil water and is relatively mobile in the plant. Deficiency is rare but may cause urea accumulation, poor seed development, leaf tip problems or reduced vigor. High pH, sandy soil, low organic matter and strong fixation can reduce it; contaminated soil can contain too much.</p>
<p>Most farms should not apply nickel routinely. Confirm with tissue or soil testing and qualified local advice. A balanced organic matter program usually supplies small amounts. Excess Ni is toxic to roots and can enter the food chain.</p>

<table><thead><tr><th>Quick reading clue</th><th>Often suggests</th><th>Important caution</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Older leaves affected first</td><td>Mobile nutrients such as N, P, K or Mg</td><td>Water stress, roots and disease can look similar.</td></tr><tr><td>Young leaves or growing points affected first</td><td>Relatively immobile nutrients such as Ca, B, Fe, Zn, Mn or Cu</td><td>Crop-specific symptoms and soil tests still matter.</td></tr><tr><td>Edges scorch</td><td>Often K, salt injury or drought</td><td>Check EC, roots and irrigation before adding K.</td></tr><tr><td>Green veins with yellow tissue</td><td>Often Fe, Mg or Mn pattern</td><td>Leaf age and soil pH separate the possibilities.</td></tr></tbody></table>

<h2 id="movement">4. How nutrients move through soil and reach roots</h2>
<p><strong>Mass flow</strong> is nutrient movement with water moving toward roots as plants transpire. Nitrate, sulfate, calcium, magnesium and some boron commonly depend strongly on this pathway. <strong>Diffusion</strong> is movement from a zone of higher concentration toward a zone of lower concentration. Phosphorus and potassium often reach roots mainly by diffusion across thin water films. <strong>Root interception</strong> occurs when a growing root physically contacts nutrient-holding soil surfaces and exchanges ions.</p>
<p>Water must be present for ions to dissolve, but too much water can remove oxygen and damage roots. In drought, soil water films become thin, diffusion slows and roots stop exploring dry pores. In waterlogging, oxygen falls, roots die and microbes change nitrogen and manganese chemistry. Heavy rainfall or irrigation can move nitrate, sulfate, chloride and boron below roots. Runoff and erosion carry dissolved or attached phosphorus and potassium away, especially from bare sloping land.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Sandy soil:</strong> large pores drain quickly, so water, nitrate, sulfate and chloride can move rapidly; nutrient holding capacity is often lower.</li><li><strong>Clay soil:</strong> small pores hold water and exchangeable nutrients, but diffusion can be slow when dry and oxygen can be low when wet. Some clay minerals fix potassium or phosphate.</li><li><strong>Organic matter:</strong> holds nutrients, improves aggregation and feeds microbes, but high-carbon residue can temporarily immobilize available nitrogen while microbes decompose it.</li><li><strong>Rainfall:</strong> can dissolve and move fertilizer, reduce surface ammonia loss if enough water enters the soil, cause runoff on sealed surfaces or leach nutrients below roots.</li><li><strong>Irrigation:</strong> can move nutrients into roots when correctly timed, but excessive flow can leach nutrients, cause runoff or add salts.</li><li><strong>Roots:</strong> release acids and carbon compounds, change pH and oxygen, feed microbes and create channels. A healthy root system changes the small zone around it, called the rhizosphere.</li></ul>
<p>A nutrient can be present but fail to reach a root because it is fixed to a mineral, held in an isolated dry pore, below the active root zone, carried away in runoff, or in a chemical form the root cannot absorb. The practical result is to manage both nutrient supply and the soil-root-water environment.</p>

<h2 id="availability">5. Availability, interactions and the danger of adding more</h2>
<p><strong>Nutrient availability</strong> is the balance between quantity, chemical form, water movement, root contact and plant demand. The same soil test result can perform differently in a wet compacted field and a well-drained field with active roots. Cation exchange capacity (CEC) is the soil's ability to hold positively charged nutrients such as K+, Ca2+, Mg2+ and NH4+ on clay and organic matter. Think of CEC as a set of storage seats: clay and organic matter provide more seats, while sand provides fewer. CEC does not mean the nutrient is automatically available; it means the soil can hold and exchange it.</p>
<p>Nutrients also interact. Nitrogen and sulfur are both needed for protein. Calcium and magnesium compete for exchange and root uptake, while excessive potassium can suppress magnesium. Excess phosphorus can reduce zinc availability. High pH reduces iron, zinc, manganese and sometimes phosphorus availability; low pH can increase aluminium and manganese toxicity and reduce molybdenum. This is why more fertilizer can create another nutrient problem. Diagnose the limiting nutrient and correct the soil condition, not just the symptom.</p>

<h2 id="products">6. Fertilizer products and label calculations</h2>
<p>Fertilizer grades are usually percentages by mass. In a label such as 46-0-0, the first number is percent nitrogen, the second is percent phosphate reported as P2O5, and the third is percent potash reported as K2O. These label conventions are not exactly the same as elemental P and K. Follow the label and local advisory system when converting to elemental nutrients.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Product</th><th>Common analysis</th><th>Useful when</th><th>Limitations and cautions</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Urea</td><td>46-0-0</td><td>A concentrated N source for planned top dressing or fertigation where suitable.</td><td>Surface losses are possible; salt and seed injury occur if concentrated. No P, K, S or Ca.</td></tr><tr><td>DAP</td><td>18-46-0</td><td>Supplies N and high P near establishing roots where a test supports P.</td><td>Not a universal starter; concentrated placement can injure seed and P can be fixed.</td></tr><tr><td>MAP</td><td>11-52-0</td><td>High P with less N; often useful for starter placement in suitable soils.</td><td>Still concentrated; P may be unavailable at high or low pH.</td></tr><tr><td>CAN</td><td>27-0-0 plus calcium on some products</td><td>N top dressing where nitrate and ammonium forms fit the crop and soil.</td><td>Check the actual label because analysis varies; supplies no P or K.</td></tr><tr><td>MOP</td><td>0-0-60</td><td>Concentrated K where chloride is acceptable and a soil test supports K.</td><td>Adds chloride and salts; not ideal for every chloride-sensitive crop.</td></tr><tr><td>SOP</td><td>0-0-50 plus about 18% S on common products</td><td>K plus sulfur where chloride should be limited.</td><td>Usually more expensive; verify analysis and quality.</td></tr><tr><td>NPK 17-17-17</td><td>17-17-17</td><td>Supplies equal labeled percentages when all three nutrients are needed.</td><td>It may over-apply one nutrient while under-supplying another.</td></tr><tr><td>NPK 15-15-15</td><td>15-15-15</td><td>A balanced compound in systems with matching soil and crop demand.</td><td>Balanced does not mean correct; no automatic S or micronutrients.</td></tr><tr><td>Lime</td><td>Not an N-P-K fertilizer</td><td>Raises acid-soil pH and may supply Ca or Mg.</td><td>Needs a pH and lime-requirement test; quality and particle size control reaction.</td></tr><tr><td>Gypsum</td><td>Calcium sulfate</td><td>Supplies Ca and S and can help selected sodic soils.</td><td>Does not normally raise pH and is not a universal soil conditioner.</td></tr><tr><td>Manure and compost</td><td>Variable</td><td>Add organic matter and release a range of nutrients.</td><td>Analysis, maturity, salt, weed seed and food-safety risk vary; not a complete guaranteed fertilizer.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h3>Simple calculations</h3>
<p>For a 100 kg application of 46-0-0 urea: <strong>100 x 0.46 = 46 kg of labeled nitrogen</strong>. For 100 kg of DAP 18-46-0: 18 kg of labeled N and 46 kg of labeled P2O5. For 100 kg of MAP 11-52-0: 11 kg N and 52 kg P2O5. For 100 kg of MOP 0-0-60: 60 kg of labeled K2O. For 100 kg of 17-17-17: 17 kg each of labeled N, P2O5 and K2O. A 50 kg bag contains half those amounts. To find product needed for a target labeled nutrient, divide the target by the decimal fraction: 46 kg N / 0.46 = 100 kg urea. This is a product calculation, not a recommendation for a crop.</p>
<p>Check whether a product is blended or compound, whether the label reports P and K as oxides, and whether secondary or micronutrients are guaranteed. Compare cost per kilogram of the nutrient actually needed, not just the price of a bag.</p>

<h2 id="four-rs">7. The 4Rs of fertilizer management</h2>
<h3>Right source</h3>
<p>The right source supplies the needed nutrient in a form that fits the crop, soil, stage, water, equipment, cost and local availability. Urea is not automatically better than CAN; DAP is not automatically the best planting fertilizer; MOP is not automatically wrong. Choose from soil test results, crop demand, previous manure, irrigation, pH and the product label. In some regions the local system uses compound products such as Zimbabwe's Compound D, C or S, Kenya's Mavuno products, South African blends, Brazil-specific NPK formulas or India-specific neem-coated urea. These are examples in the application's recommendation data, not universal prescriptions. Open the <a href="/calculators/fertilizer.html">location-aware fertilizer calculator</a> and verify the returned assumptions and source before spending money.</p>
<h3>Right rate or right amount</h3>
<p>Too little leaves a gap; the correct rate meets crop demand and soil supply at an economic return; too much can burn roots, raise salts, leach, run off, volatilize, cause lodging or create antagonism. The <strong>economic optimum</strong> is the rate where the value of the extra yield is about equal to the cost and risk of the extra nutrient, not the maximum biological yield at any price. A simple record compares product cost with harvestable yield response across a small test strip. Calibrate a spreader or cup, weigh a known area and do not estimate a 50 kg bag by eye.</p>
<h3>Right time</h3>
<p>Basal fertilizer is applied before or at planting; starter fertilizer is a small amount near young roots; top dressing is applied after establishment; split N applications divide a large requirement across crop demand. Apply when roots can use the nutrient and when moisture will move it into soil without immediate runoff or leaching. Rain-fed fields need a realistic rainfall window. Irrigated fields need measured irrigation, not just a calendar date. Nitrogen is often split before rapid vegetative growth, flowering or grain filling according to crop-specific guidance. Do not place urea on dry bare soil in extreme heat and assume it is safe: surface residence, wind, pH, residue and the chance of rain or irrigation determine volatilization risk. Rain or irrigation must be enough to move urea into soil, but intense water can cause runoff or leaching.</p>
<h3>Right place</h3>
<p><strong>Broadcasting</strong> spreads fertilizer over the whole surface and can suit some maintenance or incorporated applications. <strong>Banding or row placement</strong> puts a narrow strip beside and below the seed or row. <strong>Side dressing</strong> places fertilizer beside an established crop. <strong>Spot or ring placement</strong> puts measured fertilizer near individual plants, while keeping it away from stems. <strong>Deep placement</strong> places some material below the surface where crop and soil conditions support it. <strong>Fertigation</strong> delivers soluble nutrients through irrigation and needs filtration, compatibility and precise water management. <strong>Foliar feeding</strong> puts a small amount on leaves and can correct some micronutrients quickly, but it cannot replace root-zone fertility and can burn foliage if mixed or concentrated incorrectly.</p>
<p>Placement matters because phosphorus moves little and benefits from being near developing roots; nitrogen forms move more with soil water and can be split or side-dressed; potassium placement must respect salt risk; seed and young roots are sensitive to concentrated salts and free ammonia. The crop row, soil texture, fertilizer form, moisture, equipment and local recommendation should decide the method.</p>
<div class="notice"><strong>4R principle:</strong> Right Source + Right Rate + Right Time + Right Place = better fertilizer efficiency. The 4Rs are a decision process, not a fixed recipe.</div>

<h2 id="losses">8. Where fertilizer goes after application</h2>
<p><strong>Leaching</strong> is dissolved nutrient movement downward with water, like fertilizer being carried below the main root zone by heavy rain or excessive irrigation. Nitrate, sulfate, chloride and boron are especially mobile. <strong>Runoff</strong> is water flowing over the surface; it can carry dissolved nutrients. <strong>Erosion</strong> carries soil particles with attached phosphorus and potassium. <strong>Volatilization</strong> is a nutrient changing to a gas and leaving the soil; surface urea can lose ammonia when warm, windy, moist or alkaline conditions favour the process before incorporation or rainfall.</p>
<p><strong>Immobilization</strong> occurs when microbes temporarily take mineral nutrients, especially nitrogen, while decomposing high-carbon residue. <strong>Fixation</strong> is stronger chemical or mineral holding, such as phosphate on iron, aluminium or calcium compounds or potassium between clay layers. <strong>Denitrification</strong> is microbial conversion of nitrate to gases under low-oxygen waterlogged conditions. Plant uptake and soil storage are useful destinations, but soil storage is not the same as immediate availability. Reduce loss with soil cover, erosion control, proper placement, split applications, suitable timing, measured irrigation and healthy roots.</p>

<h2 id="diagnosis">9. Why fertilizer sometimes does not work</h2>
<h3>"I applied fertilizer, but my plants are still not growing. Why?"</h3>
<ol><li><strong>Wrong nutrient or product:</strong> a balanced NPK cannot correct a confirmed sulfur, zinc or pH problem if the needed nutrient is absent or unavailable.</li><li><strong>Wrong rate, time or place:</strong> under-application fails; excess burns or salts; late application cannot repair lost yield; seed contact injures roots.</li><li><strong>pH or fixation:</strong> acid or alkaline chemistry can lock nutrients or cause toxicity.</li><li><strong>Water problem:</strong> dry soil stops movement; waterlogging kills roots; excessive irrigation leaches nitrate and adds salts.</li><li><strong>Root damage:</strong> compaction, hardpan, insects, disease, nematodes or fertilizer burn can stop uptake.</li><li><strong>Salinity or sodicity:</strong> roots may be unable to take up water even though fertilizer is present.</li><li><strong>Poor quality or measurement:</strong> wrong product, damp granules, counterfeit material, blocked spreader or miscalculated area changes the dose.</li><li><strong>Antagonism:</strong> too much P can reduce Zn; too much K can reduce Mg; excess one cation can limit another.</li><li><strong>Another diagnosis:</strong> pests, disease, herbicide injury, drought, cold, shading or a virus can resemble nutrient deficiency.</li></ol>
<p>Investigate in this order: compare good and poor patches; ask which leaves are affected; inspect roots and soil moisture; review product, amount, date, placement and weather; test soil pH and nutrients; use tissue testing where available; then make a small, measured correction and record the response. A single visual symptom is a clue, not proof.</p>

<h2 id="ph">10. Understanding soil pH</h2>
<p><strong>Soil pH</strong> describes how acidic or alkaline the soil solution is. It uses a logarithmic scale: each whole number represents a tenfold change in hydrogen-ion activity. Around pH 7 is neutral; below 7 is acidic; above 7 is alkaline. Many crops perform reasonably near slightly acid to near-neutral conditions, but the best range varies by crop, variety and soil.</p>
<p>pH controls chemical form, microbial activity, root growth and fertilizer efficiency. Acid soil can release toxic aluminium and manganese, reduce calcium, magnesium, phosphorus or molybdenum availability, and increase leaching. High pH can reduce iron, zinc, manganese, copper and phosphorus availability. Microbes that decompose residues, fix nitrogen or transform nutrients also respond to pH. Roots may be short, brown and poorly branched where acidity, aluminium, compaction or salts are severe.</p>
<h3>Acidic soil</h3>
<p>Acidity develops from rainfall leaching basic cations, crop removal, acid-forming fertilizers, organic matter processes and parent material. Symptoms can include stunting, poor nodulation, shallow roots, patchy growth and aluminium toxicity, but symptoms alone cannot confirm acidity. Sample representative areas and request pH, exchangeable aluminium, calcium, magnesium, CEC and a lime requirement where relevant. Do not apply lime because one plant is yellow.</p>
<h3>Agricultural lime</h3>
<p>Agricultural lime is usually ground calcium carbonate. It neutralizes acidity, reduces aluminium toxicity, supplies calcium and can improve phosphorus and microbial conditions. Dolomitic lime contains calcium magnesium carbonate and is useful when soil is acid and magnesium is also low. Lime is an amendment, not a general NPK fertilizer. The required amount depends on pH, buffering capacity, texture, CEC, target crop and product quality.</p>
<p><strong>Neutralizing value</strong> compares how much acid a lime product can neutralize. <strong>Particle size</strong> controls reaction speed: fine particles react faster, while coarse particles last longer. Read the guaranteed analysis and effective neutralizing value. Incorporation mixes lime through the worked layer and usually reacts more uniformly; surface lime can work more slowly and is useful in established systems where tillage is not suitable. It may take months or longer to change a deeper soil. Lime should not be applied blindly, placed against seed, or confused with gypsum. Test, calculate from the report, spread uniformly, incorporate where appropriate and record the date.</p>

<h2 id="alkaline">11. Alkaline soils</h2>
<p>Alkaline soil generally has pH above 7, although the exact management problem may be carbonate, salinity, sodicity, irrigation water or a mixture. High pH can make iron, zinc, manganese, copper and phosphorus less available. Young leaves may be yellow between green veins, plants may be stunted and roots may be restricted. Confirm pH with a representative test and add EC, carbonate, sodium and nutrient results where the field suggests them.</p>
<p>Management depends on the cause: improve organic matter and root activity, select tolerant crops or varieties, use locally suitable acidifying or chelated products, manage irrigation water, and place the right nutrient near active roots. Elemental sulfur reacts slowly through microbes and should not be used as a quick universal acidifier. Adding more micronutrient fertilizer without addressing high pH, bicarbonate water or roots can waste money. Do not confuse alkaline soil with saline or sodic soil; pH alone does not diagnose either.</p>

<h2 id="saline">12. Saline soil</h2>
<p><strong>Salinity</strong> means too many dissolved salts in soil water. Salts accumulate when irrigation water contains salts, evaporation is high, drainage is poor, a shallow water table rises, or repeated irrigation leaves salts behind. Plants experience <strong>osmotic stress</strong>: the soil solution holds water so strongly that roots struggle to take it up even when soil looks wet. Specific ions such as sodium, chloride or boron can also become toxic, and salt can disturb nutrient balance.</p>
<p>Electrical conductivity (EC) is a simple measure of how well the soil-water extract conducts electricity; more dissolved salts usually means higher EC. Use the laboratory's crop-specific interpretation rather than one universal cutoff. Look for poor emergence, uneven patches, leaf tip burn, stunting and plants that wilt despite wet soil, then test soil EC and irrigation-water EC. Management may include drainage, good-quality water, measured leaching below the root zone, raised beds, reduced evaporation, salt-tolerant crops and avoiding unnecessary fertilizer salts. Leaching without drainage can move salts into the water table or another field. Fertilizer itself adds soluble salts, so use the smallest effective rate and avoid concentrated placement.</p>

<h2 id="sodic">13. Sodic soil</h2>
<p><strong>Sodicity</strong> means too much exchangeable sodium on soil particle surfaces. In simple terms, sodium pushes calcium and other helpful ions away and makes clay particles disperse. The soil surface seals, water enters slowly, the soil can be hard when dry and sticky when wet, and roots lose both air and usable space. Sodic soil is not the same as saline soil: saline soil has high dissolved salts; sodic soil has a damaging sodium balance. A soil can be saline, sodic, both or neither.</p>
<p>Confirm the problem with laboratory EC, exchangeable sodium percentage or related sodium tests, CEC and sometimes saturated hydraulic conductivity. Symptoms include ponding, runoff, crusting, poor emergence, sparse roots and nutrient uptake problems. Gypsum can supply soluble calcium that replaces sodium on exchange sites, but it is appropriate only when the test shows a gypsum-responsive condition. After calcium replaces sodium, good-quality water and drainage are needed to leach sodium below the root zone. Organic matter, surface cover, careful traffic and improved infiltration help structure, but they do not remove sodium by themselves. Avoid poor-quality irrigation water and do not assume gypsum will solve compaction, salinity, acid soil or every crust.</p>

<h2 id="crusting">14. Soil crusting or soil cap</h2>
<p>A soil crust, sometimes called a soil cap, is a thin hard surface layer formed when heavy raindrops or irrigation break aggregates, splash fine particles into pores, seal the surface and then leave it hard as it dries. Fine silt, bare soil, low organic matter and intense rain increase risk. Farmers may see a smooth plate or hard skin across the bed.</p>
<p>Crusting reduces infiltration, increases runoff and erosion, limits oxygen exchange and makes seedlings spend their energy pushing through a hard layer. It can produce poor or delayed emergence and uneven stands. Prevent it with residue, mulch, mature compost, cover crops, living roots, reduced unnecessary disturbance and irrigation that does not strike the soil violently. Break a shallow crust carefully when crop and moisture permit, but avoid creating more fine dust or compacting wet soil. Good soil structure is the long-term answer; repeated aggressive cultivation can make the next crust worse.</p>

<h2 id="hardpan">15. Soil pan or hardpan</h2>
<p>A <strong>hardpan</strong> is a dense layer that roots and water cannot easily pass. It may be natural, formed by clay accumulation or cemented minerals, or caused by machinery, repeated livestock traffic or a plough pan at the same depth. Dig beside a poor plant, push a rod through moist soil and compare good and poor areas. Look for roots turning sideways, stopping at a flat layer, water standing above it, or a dry layer below it.</p>
<p>A pan can cause waterlogging above the layer after rain and drought below it because roots cannot reach stored water. Restricted roots then reduce nutrient uptake even when fertilizer is present. Deep ripping or subsoiling can help only when a real, continuous restrictive layer is confirmed, soil moisture is suitable, equipment can reach the depth and the result will last. Ripping wet soil can smear the layer; ripping dry soil can require excessive power; traffic can recreate it. Combine justified loosening with controlled traffic, organic matter, deep-rooted crops, surface cover, drainage and avoidance of unnecessary passes.</p>

<h2 id="organic">16. Organic matter and fertilizer</h2>
<p>Organic matter is the living, dead and decomposing material from roots, residues, manure, compost and soil organisms. It helps bind particles into aggregates, improves water storage and infiltration, feeds microbes, holds exchangeable nutrients and can increase CEC. It releases nutrients gradually as microbes decompose it and can improve fertilizer efficiency by reducing runoff and keeping roots active.</p>
<p>Compost, manure, crop residues, cover crops and green manures are valuable, but their nutrient content is variable. Test manure or use a reliable analysis where possible. Fresh manure can burn plants, add salts, carry pathogens and weed seeds, and create food-safety risks. High-carbon residues can temporarily immobilize nitrogen. Organic matter is not a guaranteed substitute for every nutrient: a crop may still require a measured mineral source of N, P, K, S or micronutrients. Recycle safe residues, protect soil cover and account for the nutrients organic inputs contribute.</p>

<h2 id="low-cost">17. Increase production without wasting money</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Test before buying:</strong> sample uniform areas and test pH and nutrients.</li><li><strong>Correct the biggest constraint:</strong> pH, drainage, compaction, salinity, weeds or roots may return more than extra fertilizer.</li><li><strong>Prioritize limiting nutrients:</strong> do not buy a balanced product just because it is familiar.</li><li><strong>Use the 4Rs:</strong> select the product, amount, time and placement that match the crop.</li><li><strong>Split nitrogen where appropriate:</strong> reduce exposure to leaching or volatilization and match demand.</li><li><strong>Use organic resources intelligently:</strong> record manure and compost quantities and test them when possible.</li><li><strong>Calibrate:</strong> weigh product over a measured area and check spreader, planter or fertigation output.</li><li><strong>Protect water:</strong> repair leaks, prevent runoff, maintain soil cover and irrigate the root zone.</li><li><strong>Keep records:</strong> field, crop, product, analysis, amount, date, placement, weather, cost, yield and plant response.</li><li><strong>Compare return:</strong> use the <a href="/calculators/farm-budget.html">farm budget</a> and <a href="/calculators/farm-profit.html">farm profit calculator</a> to compare fertilizer cost with saleable yield, not just total harvest.</li></ol>
<p>The cheapest input is not always the cheapest production. A smaller correct application that the crop can use is often better than a large application that is lost. Use the <a href="/calculators/fertilizer.html">fertilizer calculator</a> for a structured estimate, and treat it as a planning aid rather than a replacement for soil testing or local guidance.</p>

<h2 id="locations">18. Location-aware examples and unit conversion</h2>
<p>The application's fertilizer data includes Zimbabwe, Kenya, South Africa, Brazil, Nigeria, Zambia, Ghana, Tanzania, Ethiopia, Uganda, the United States and India, with country defaults such as hectares or acres and local bag sizes. Those defaults help organize a recommendation, but country and region do not replace a soil test. A Natural Region II field in Zimbabwe, a Rift Valley field in Kenya, a Cerrado field in Brazil and a Midwest field in the United States can grow different crops on different soils under different rainfall and yield targets.</p>
<p><strong>Example rate - not a universal recommendation:</strong> suppose an adviser has verified a soil-test recommendation for a maize field in a named region. Enter the country, region, crop, stage, production system and test results in the existing calculator, then check the source and product analysis. Do not copy a Zimbabwe example into Ghana, or a Kenyan bag rate into India. The calculator's sources include FAO, Zimbabwe DR&amp;SS, KALRO, ARC South Africa, Embrapa, USDA/land-grant extension, ICAR and IITA. Use the source attached to the returned recommendation and confirm current local advice.</p>
<h3>Conversions</h3>
<ul><li>1 hectare = 10,000 square metres.</li><li>1 acre = about 0.4047 hectare; 1 hectare = about 2.471 acres.</li><li>kg/acre = kg/ha x 0.4047.</li><li>kg/ha = kg/acre x 2.471.</li><li>Bags/ha = kg/ha divided by the bag mass in kg.</li><li>Bags/acre = kg/acre divided by the bag mass in kg.</li></ul>
<p>For example, if a confirmed plan says 250 kg/ha and the local bag is 50 kg, that equals 5 bags/ha. If the same product is expressed as 101.2 kg/acre, a 50 kg bag equals about 2.0 bags/acre. This is only a unit conversion; it does not prove that 250 kg/ha is correct. India may use 45 kg urea bags in the application's defaults, while the United States may use a 22.68 kg default for 50 lb bags. Always use the actual bag label.</p>

<h2 id="scenarios">19. Farmer troubleshooting scenarios</h2>
<h3>Scenario 1: "I applied urea but the crop is still yellow."</h3>
<p>Check whether the yellowing starts on older leaves, whether soil is dry or waterlogged, whether the rate and area were measured, and whether urea stayed on the surface. Inspect roots, ask what rain or irrigation followed, and test pH and nitrogen where possible. The cause may be leaching, volatilization, poor roots, disease, sulfur shortage or a non-nutrient problem. Do not automatically repeat urea.</p>
<h3>Scenario 2: "I applied DAP but the roots are still poor."</h3>
<p>Phosphorus may be fixed by pH and soil minerals, placed too far from roots, applied to dry or cold soil, or made ineffective by compaction and waterlogging. Inspect root depth and soil structure. Check pH and available P, and remember that root disease or aluminium toxicity can look like P deficiency. A second DAP application can worsen salt or P imbalance.</p>
<h3>Scenario 3: "My fertilizer is on the field but the crop is not using it."</h3>
<p>Fertilizer must dissolve, move to a living root and remain in a form the root can take up. Ask whether there is moisture, oxygen, active roots, suitable pH and enough time. Look for leaching, runoff, fixation, volatilization, salinity and compaction. The soil-root-water relationship explains why a visible granule is not proof of plant access.</p>
<h3>Scenario 4: "My soil is acidic."</h3>
<p>Confirm pH with a representative laboratory sample and request lime requirement and aluminium information where relevant. Check crop tolerance, calcium, magnesium, P and CEC. Use agricultural or dolomitic lime only at the calculated rate, spread uniformly and allow time to react. Gypsum is not a replacement for lime when the problem is acidity.</p>
<h3>Scenario 5: "My soil is alkaline."</h3>
<p>Test pH, EC, carbonate and nutrients, and check irrigation water. Iron or zinc fertilizer alone may fail if high pH prevents uptake. Improve root conditions, use suitable forms or chelates and follow a local plan. Do not acidify a whole field blindly.</p>
<h3>Scenario 6: "My field becomes hard after rain."</h3>
<p>Look for a thin surface cap, fine particles, bare soil and raindrop impact. This suggests crusting; mulch, residues, organic matter, cover and gentler irrigation can help. If hardness extends deeper and roots stop at a layer, investigate compaction or hardpan rather than treating it as crusting.</p>
<h3>Scenario 7: "Water stands in one part of my field."</h3>
<p>Compare slope, irrigation flow, wheel tracks, a hard layer, blocked drains and soil texture. Dig carefully and inspect roots. Waterlogging, compaction and a pan can restrict nutrient uptake. Repair the cause and adjust irrigation before increasing fertilizer.</p>
<h3>Scenario 8: "I spend a lot on fertilizer but yields remain low."</h3>
<p>Build a field history: crop, yield, pH, soil test, products, amounts, dates, placement, water, weather and root observations. Compare a good strip with a poor strip. Check seed, population, weeds, pests, diseases, drainage, compaction, salinity and market timing. Run a small measured nutrient trial only when the diagnosis supports it, and calculate the yield value against input cost using the farm budget.</p>

<h2 id="checklist">20. Final farmer checklist</h2>
<p>Before applying fertilizer, ask:</p>
<ol><li>What does my crop need?</li><li>What nutrients are already in my soil?</li><li>Have I tested my soil?</li><li>Is my soil pH suitable?</li><li>What fertilizer source supplies the required nutrient?</li><li>What is the correct amount?</li><li>When should I apply it?</li><li>Where should I place it?</li><li>Will there be enough soil moisture?</li><li>Could the fertilizer be lost?</li><li>Could I be creating a nutrient imbalance?</li><li>Can I achieve the same result more cheaply?</li><li>How will I measure the result?</li><li>What does the crop tell me after application?</li></ol>
<p>After application, record the product analysis, amount, treated area, date, placement, weather and crop stage. Return to the field and ask: which leaves changed, are new leaves healthy, are roots white and active, is the soil too wet or dry, and did the crop response match the diagnosis?</p>
<div class="notice"><strong>Final principle:</strong> The goal is not to use more fertilizer. The goal is to use the right nutrient, in the right amount, at the right time and in the right place so the crop can actually use it. <strong>Right Source + Right Rate + Right Time + Right Place = Better Fertilizer Efficiency.</strong></div>
<p>For connected decisions, review <a href="/guides/understanding-your-soil.html">Understanding Your Soil</a>, <a href="/guides/understanding-soil-ph.html">Understanding Soil pH</a>, the <a href="/guides/beginner-irrigation-guide.html">irrigation guide</a>, <a href="/guides/reduce-water-waste.html">water-waste guide</a> and <a href="/guides/prepare-soil-for-vegetables.html">soil preparation guide</a>. Use the <a href="/calculators/crop-spacing.html">crop spacing calculator</a> when plant population changes the nutrient demand per area. For systematic soil and fertilizer decisions, use the <a href="/decision-tools/soil-needs/">What Does My Soil Need?</a> decision tool.</p>`;
}
